"A storage partition diagram shows how a logical dataset maps to partition ranges, files, and internal storage groups.\n\nUse when: Explain pruning and physical organization of large datasets. Review partition boundaries and file-level layout choices.\n\nAvoid when: Readers need only entity relationships or task dependencies.\n\nVisual grammar: Nested groups encode dataset, partition, file, and optional row-group levels. Range or value labels specify partition membership. Selected scan paths identify included and pruned regions without inventing performance claims.\n\nBuild: Choose the storage engine or file format and snapshot revision. Declare partition expressions and boundary conventions. Expand representative files or row groups only where needed. Lay out hierarchy levels with padded boundaries and short range labels.\n\nCheck: Partition ranges are disjoint or explicitly overlapping as designed. Inclusive and exclusive endpoints are clear. Physical files are distinguished from logical partition definitions. Nested titles and scan arrows never overlap file or range text.\n\nPitfalls: Assuming directory names alone prove engine-level partition pruning. Drawing equal-size boxes that imply equal byte sizes without evidence.\n\nExample: Events partition by month; one selected month expands into Parquet files with column-oriented row groups.\n\nAlternatives: Query execution plan, Wide-column data model."
